2.0.0 (2026):

64-bit port (LP64, tested on x86_64 Linux) and robustness work for
ncacn_ip_tcp servers and clients.  The version number changes because the
ABI changed.

- The RPC runtime, the NDR marshaling code and the IDL compiler work on
  LP64 (pointer and long sizes, alignment, union discriminants, full
  pointers, format strings).
- Builds with current toolchains (C23 IDL compiler; tested with gcc 13 to
  16, autoconf 2.69 to 2.73), in tree and out of tree, parallel make;
  "make check" in dcethreads, the perf suite, the new load test
  (tests/loadtest) and the error text test (tests/errtext).
- Packages (packaging/): .deb for Debian 13, Ubuntu 24.04 and 26.04, .rpm
  for Fedora, RHEL/AlmaLinux/Rocky 9 and 10, openSUSE Tumbleweed and Leap
  16.0, and an Arch Linux package; installed below /opt/dce with a systemd
  unit for rpcd.
- dcethreads delivers DCE cancels itself instead of through NPTL
  cancellation (DCETHREADS_CANCEL=nptl selects the old code); lost wakeup
  in the condition wait fixed.  The wake-up signal can be changed
  (DCETHREADS_WAKE_SIG).
- The UUID functions are part of libdcerpc (there is no DCE libuuid any
  more; do not link -luuid); libdcerpc symbols are versioned DCERPC_1.1.
- Connections: poll listener without the FD_SETSIZE limit, accept backoff,
  immediate restart of servers with a well-known endpoint, context rundown
  for killed clients and vanished client hosts, idle connection reclaim
  (RPC_CN_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT), client bind timeout, per-connection
  keepalive and user timeout (RPC_CN_KEEPALIVE, RPC_CN_USER_TIMEOUT).
- A cancel now also ends a call whose send is blocked because the server
  stopped reading (after the cancel timeout, like any other cancel), and
  timers set by application threads no longer expire early (cancel
  timeout, call timeouts).
- Network input is checked before it is used: the CN and DG packet
  headers and bodies, the NDR unmarshaling of arrays, strings, pointers,
  unions and pipes, protocol towers, and the endpoint map requests of rpcd.
  The fixes come from fuzzing these paths under ASan and UBSan.  They
  include a stack buffer overflow and an unbounded allocation in rpcd that
  unauthenticated clients could trigger over the network (port 135).
- dce_error_inq_text() has a built-in text for every status code (a
  message catalog through NLSPATH still takes precedence).
- ncalrpc: the sockets are in one directory (/tmp/.ncalrpc by default,
  configure --with-ncalrpc-dir; /run/freedce/ncalrpc in the packages),
  dynamic endpoints get unique names, and no socket files are left behind.
- rpc_ep_register works after rpc_server_use_all_protseqs.
- Many runtime fixes found by the tests, ASan, UBSan and valgrind (maybe
  calls, liveness monitoring lock, association group reference counts,
  status exceptions, UDP cancels, uninitialized bytes in PDUs).
- The license of every file is recorded in packaging/debian/copyright,
  with an overview in COPYING and the license texts in LICENSES/.

Not built by default and not tested: the authentication modules (NTLMSSP,
GSS) and code set conversion.  The UDP broadcast test of the perf suite is
skipped by "make check" unless PERF_BROADCAST=1.

See README for the build and the runtime settings.

DCOM:

We (well, me mainly) are beginning to support DCOM/Object RPC as documented by Microsoft.

The aim is to produce a free implementation of DCOM that can be installed by
anyone on anyone's Linux box without a heavy license fee.  People can then
really think about using it for their applications and then we might find a
whole host of component-orientated applications start to spring up, and then
we might find a lot of components being created to make the applications
easier to develop, and the range of applications will grow and grow...

For more info on this, subscribe to the mailing list:
	freedce-dcomdev@lists.sourceforge.net

